Cold brew is simple and cheap to make. The one catch is that it takes time, usually most of a day. But nearly all of that is waiting, and you end up with coffee ready to go in the fridge for days.

This is part four of [Brew Methods](/coffee/guides/coffee-brew-methods/).

## Cold brew vs. iced coffee

They're not the same thing. **Iced coffee** is hot coffee that's been cooled down or poured over ice. **Cold brew** never touches hot water at all. The grounds steep in cold or room-temperature water for many hours.

Cold water pulls flavor out slowly and leaves a lot of the acidity and bitterness behind. So cold brew comes out smooth, low in acidity, a little sweet and chocolatey, and heavy in body. The trade-off is that the bright, fruity notes of a light roast mostly don't make it through, so medium and darker roasts are usually the better pick.

## Ratio and steep time

Most people make cold brew as a **concentrate**, strong enough that you dilute it when you pour a glass. Around **5:1 to 8:1** (water to coffee) is the usual range. It takes less room in the fridge, and you can make each glass as strong as you want.

Steep time is the other lever:

- **Under about 12 hours:** Usually weak and a little flat.
- **12 to 18 hours on the counter, or 18 to 24 in the fridge:** The sweet spot for most coffees.
- **Much past 24 hours:** It can start to taste woody or bitter.

